1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,725
Subtitles show you what someone said.

2
00:00:02,725 --> 00:00:06,396
They don't show you which word they're saying right now.

3
00:00:07,167 --> 00:00:08,375
EveryWord does.

4
00:00:08,375 --> 00:00:11,759
This is a Fire TV, Amazon's hosted device,

5
00:00:11,759 --> 00:00:16,029
playing Sintel with the subtitles its makers shipped.

6
00:00:16,029 --> 00:00:19,090
EveryWord added the time of each word,

7
00:00:19,090 --> 00:00:22,071
and the film became reading practice.

8
00:00:23,133 --> 00:00:25,221
Pick a story with the remote,

9
00:00:25,221 --> 00:00:27,813
and each word lights as it's spoken.

10
00:00:33,133 --> 00:00:37,313
The television also reports the words played to our MCP

11
00:00:37,313 --> 00:00:37,845
server,

12
00:00:37,845 --> 00:00:41,797
so an assistant can answer "how far did my child get

13
00:00:41,797 --> 00:00:45,901
today?" from playback activity the TV itself recorded.

14
00:00:47,033 --> 00:00:50,855
Amazon Transcribe never decides which words appear.

15
00:00:50,855 --> 00:00:52,653
The subtitle author did.

16
00:00:52,653 --> 00:00:55,650
Transcribe only says when to light them,

17
00:00:55,650 --> 00:00:57,898
so even if it mishears a word,

18
00:00:57,898 --> 00:01:00,521
it can't change what a child reads.

19
00:01:01,267 --> 00:01:03,870
The demo is a film and five fables,

20
00:01:03,870 --> 00:01:06,399
but the system isn't tied to them.

21
00:01:06,399 --> 00:01:08,705
Give it a video with subtitles,

22
00:01:08,705 --> 00:01:11,532
and Amazon Transcribe adds the timing.

23
00:01:11,532 --> 00:01:13,540
Give it public-domain text,

24
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:16,441
and Amazon Polly narrates and times it.

25
00:01:16,441 --> 00:01:20,011
Both produce the same word-level caption format.

26
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:24,984
Karaoke-style subtitles ran on Indian national television

27
00:01:24,984 --> 00:01:26,130
for two decades,

28
00:01:26,130 --> 00:01:29,713
reaching an estimated two hundred million viewers.

29
00:01:29,713 --> 00:01:31,218
In a five-year study,

30
00:01:31,218 --> 00:01:35,087
thirty-two percentage points more children became good

31
00:01:35,087 --> 00:01:35,660
readers.

32
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:40,345
It worked because nobody had to choose it: the practice

33
00:01:40,345 --> 00:01:43,214
rode programmes people already watched.

34
00:01:43,214 --> 00:01:46,891
EveryWord makes the idea programmable: upgrade the

35
00:01:46,891 --> 00:01:48,950
subtitles video already has,

36
00:01:48,950 --> 00:01:52,260
instead of producing special reading content.

37
00:01:53,167 --> 00:01:56,981
Against gold word alignments on speech we didn't record,

38
00:01:56,981 --> 00:02:00,182
the median timing error is thirty milliseconds,

39
00:02:00,182 --> 00:02:02,975
and in seven hundred and sixty-six words,

40
00:02:02,975 --> 00:02:06,652
not one lit more than a hundred and fifty milliseconds

41
00:02:06,652 --> 00:02:07,061
early.

42
00:02:07,061 --> 00:02:08,559
For a learning reader,

43
00:02:08,559 --> 00:02:11,965
lighting the next word too early creates the wrong

44
00:02:11,965 --> 00:02:13,327
word-to-sound match.

45
00:02:14,067 --> 00:02:16,616
And because it's designed for children,

46
00:02:16,616 --> 00:02:19,884
it keeps only five fields of reading-session data.

47
00:02:19,884 --> 00:02:21,387
There is no microphone.

48
00:02:22,633 --> 00:02:26,359
EveryWord doesn't ask anyone to open a reading app or

49
00:02:26,359 --> 00:02:27,976
change what they watch.

50
00:02:27,976 --> 00:02:31,490
It turns the video they already chose into reading

51
00:02:31,490 --> 00:02:32,123
practice.

52
00:02:32,123 --> 00:02:35,779
And because the caption engine and renderer are open

53
00:02:35,779 --> 00:02:36,271
source,

54
00:02:36,271 --> 00:02:40,137
other developers can bring the same experience to their

55
00:02:40,137 --> 00:02:40,981
own content.

56
00:02:40,981 --> 00:02:41,684
EveryWord.

57
00:02:41,684 --> 00:02:43,441
Watching becomes reading.
